# CircuitPython Conventions

## Runtime Environment

Code runs on CircuitPython (not CPython). Key constraints:
- Limited RAM — avoid large allocations; prefer small bitmaps and streaming
- No pip/venv — libraries are built into the firmware or copied to `CIRCUITPY/lib/`
- Entry point is `code.py` on the CIRCUITPY drive; auto-reloads on save

## Core Libraries

| Module | Purpose |
|--------|---------|
| `board` | Pin definitions for Adafruit Qualia S3 (includes `TFT_PINS`, `TFT_IO_EXPANDER`) |
| `busio` | I2C bus access (`busio.I2C`) |
| `displayio` | Bitmap, TileGrid, Group, ColorConverter abstractions |
| `framebufferio` | `FramebufferDisplay` for DotClock-backed displays |
| `dotclockframebuffer` | Parallel RGB TTL framebuffer and `ioexpander_send_init_sequence` |
| `microcontroller` | Low-level pin access for debugging |

## Display Initialization Pattern

```python
from displayio import release_displays
release_displays()  # always call first

i2c = busio.I2C(board.SCL, board.SDA, frequency=400_000)
tft_io_expander = dict(board.TFT_IO_EXPANDER)
dotclockframebuffer.ioexpander_send_init_sequence(i2c, init_sequence, **tft_io_expander)
i2c.deinit()

fb = dotclockframebuffer.DotClockFramebuffer(**dict(board.TFT_PINS), **tft_timings)
display = FramebufferDisplay(fb, auto_refresh=False)
```

## Guidelines

- Always call `release_displays()` before creating a new display
- Deinit I2C after sending the init sequence (the display uses parallel RGB after init)
- Use `auto_refresh=False` during bitmap writes, then set `True` to flush
- Color format is RGB565 (16-bit) for bitmap pixel values
- Rev B boards use IO expander address `0x38`; Rev A uses the default from `board.TFT_IO_EXPANDER`
